<header><p class="st-kicker">republic · Poland, Lithuania and Russia</p><h1 class="st-title">Russia</h1><p class="st-meta">1991 CE – present · capital Moscow</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">The world's largest country, successor to the Russian SFSR in 1991, governed under the 1993 constitution and at war with Ukraine since 2014.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>The Russian Federation emerged from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dissolution-of-the-soviet-union">dissolution of the Soviet Union</a> in 1991. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mikhail-gorbachev">Mikhail Gorbachev</a>'s glasnost and perestroika had set off nationalist and separatist movements, and in June 1991 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/boris-yeltsin">Boris Yeltsin</a> became the first directly elected president in Russian history, as president of the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/russian-sfsr">Russian SFSR</a>. A coup attempt in August 1991 instead ended the Communist Party of the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/soviet-union">Soviet Union</a>, which was dissolved, and on 25 December 1991 Russia and fourteen other post-Soviet states emerged.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>Reforms along the lines of shock therapy brought privatisation, the rise of oligarchs and a deep depression. In late 1993 a constitutional crisis between Yeltsin and parliament ended violently, with over 100 people killed, and a referendum in December approved a constitution giving the president enormous powers. Armed conflict in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/north-caucasus">North Caucasus</a> and the 1998 financial crisis marked the decade. Yeltsin resigned on 31 December 1999 and handed the post to Vladimir Putin, who won the 2000 election. In 2008 Putin became prime minister and Dmitry Medvedev president. Some political scientists have characterised Putin as the head of a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dictatorship">dictatorship</a>.</p><h2>To the present</h2><p>Russian forces invaded <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/georgia">Georgia</a> from 1 to 16 August 2008 and occupied Abkhazia and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/south-ossetia">South Ossetia</a>. In 2014 Russia annexed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/crimea">Crimea</a> and supported separatists in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/donbas">Donbas</a>, and on 24 February 2022 it invaded the rest of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ukraine">Ukraine</a>. In September 2022 it proclaimed the annexation of four partly occupied regions, which was internationally denounced as illegal. The International Criminal Court issued an arrest warrant for Putin in March 2023.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/russia.jpg" alt="Russia" width="330" height="220"><figcaption>Image: Peter the Great · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Flag_of_Russia.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1991 CE – present</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a> – <a href="/centuries/21st-century">21st century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>republic</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>bureaucratic</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Moscow</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Boris Yeltsin, Vladimir Putin, Dmitry Medvedev</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Eastern Orthodox Christianity, Islam, Judaism, Buddhism</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Russian</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/russia">Russia</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Russian Federation</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=2008&amp;focus=russia" aria-label="Russia on the atlas"><svg class="map map-globe map-loc" viewBox="0 0 10000 10000" role="img" aria-label="Globe: where Russia lay"><image href="/maps/globes/g030n60.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="10000"/><path class="map-tie" d="M5366 5342L5366 5342M5366 5342L5366 5342"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M5366 5342h0M5366 5342h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5366 5342h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5366 5342h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks Moscow. <a href="/atlas?year=2008&amp;focus=russia">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>
